## 2. Background and Development

Kelvin C. Abraham is an independent researcher and engineer based in Australia. He has worked on the Tetryonic framework since approximately 2008, producing what grew into an extensive self-published corpus spanning quantum mechanics, electrodynamics, chemistry, cosmology, and mathematics.

Abraham has made his complete works freely available online, including through his website **tetryonics.com** and through Academia.edu, where multiple volumes are accessible for download. He has also produced an extended series of educational videos through the **Tetryonics** YouTube channel, under the channel name *Tetryonics University*, in which he works through derivations and applications of the framework in lecture format.

Over approximately two decades, Abraham developed the theory independently and without institutional affiliation or consistent external funding. The body of work encompasses derivations of particle masses, atomic topologies for all 120 elements, corrections to atomic weight calculations, geometric models of nuclear forces, and a reinterpretation of the periodic table based on Deuterium as the foundational nuclear unit.

A corrected value for Planck's constant, derived by Abraham from geometric first principles and Avogadro's number, appears throughout the works:

**h = 6.629432672 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s**

This value differs from the CODATA 2018 SI-defined value of 6.626070150 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s by approximately 0.05%, a discrepancy Abraham attributes to the omission of a two-dimensional equilateral geometric correction factor in the SI measurement methodology.

