## 5. Particle Topology

### 5.1 The Zero Point Field

The Zero Point Field (ZPF) is the fundamental quantum of energy. It is an equilateral triangular field with height l_q and side length L. A positive ZPF has clockwise inductive energy flux; a negative ZPF has counter-clockwise flux. These are modeled as ideal quantum inductors that store energy without dissipation until released through weak interaction.

Spin correspondence: A single ZPF face closes at 180° rotation → **spin 2**.

### 5.2 The Photon

A photon is composed of two ZPF quanta in a rhombus (diamond) configuration — one positive and one negative — forming a net-neutral 2π geometry. The photon carries EM mass but no Matter topology. Abraham identifies the ground-state relationship:

ν_z = (1/2) × f_photon → 2hν = hf

Spin: 360° closure → **spin 1**.

### 5.3 The Tetryon

The Tetryon is four ZPF quanta assembled into a tetrahedron — the minimum closed 3D volume. It is the fundamental quantum of Matter. All observable particles are integer multiples of Tetryons.

- 4 faces × 180° = 720° total angular content
- Rotational closure: 360° → **spin 1**
- Matter formula: M₀ = 4hν/c⁴
- Volume: V_T = L³ / (6√2)

### 5.4 Quarks

Quarks consist of 12 ZPF quanta (three Tetryons) with octahedral 3D topology. The six quark varieties arise from charge distribution across the 12 fascia:

| Quark | Charge | Quanta distribution |
|---|---|---|
| Up | +2/3 | [10-2] |
| Down | -1/3 | [4-8] |
| Anti-up | -2/3 | [2-10] |
| Anti-down | +1/3 | [8-4] |

Abraham states that quarks are not fundamental; they are composed of three Tetryons each, and each Tetryon carries one-third of the quark's fractional charge.

### 5.5 Leptons

Leptons also consist of 12 ZPF quanta (three Tetryons) but with dodecahedral topology, producing integer charge. The distinction from quarks is topological:

- Quarks: attractive strong force → octahedral topology
- Leptons: repulsive strong force → dodecahedral topology

The electron has rest mass 8.851486361 × 10⁻³¹ kg = 12 × m_n, consistent with the accepted value of 9.109 × 10⁻³¹ kg at the Tetryonic scale.

### 5.6 Baryons

Baryons (proton, neutron) are tri-quark assemblies with 36 ZPF quanta total:

| Particle | Distribution | Charge | Planck quanta |
|---|---|---|---|
| Proton | [24-12] | +1 | 22,500 |
| Neutron | [18-18] | 0 | 22,500 |
| Anti-proton | [12-24] | -1 | 22,500 |

Abraham identifies the correct quark arrangement of the proton as [u-d-u], not [u-u-d] as conventionally stated, based on charge geometry requirements.

**Gluons**, in Tetryonic theory, are neutral Tetryons ([2-2] configuration) located between charged Tetryons within baryon topologies. The proton contains 4 neutral Tetryons; the neutron contains 5.

### 5.7 Atomic Nuclei and the Deuterium Hypothesis

Abraham identifies Deuterium, not Hydrogen, as the foundational building block of all periodic elements. Every element is an integer multiple of the Deuterium topology (84π per atom, 72π per nucleus). All elements have equal numbers of protons, neutrons, and electrons (N = Z for all elements). Abraham argues that apparent excess neutrons in the standard model arise from an incorrect topological model of the nucleus.

