## 7. Key Predictions and Consistency Claims

### 7.1 Proton Radius Puzzle

The so-called Proton Radius Puzzle — the discrepancy between the proton radius measured via muonic hydrogen (0.8409 fm) and via electron scattering (0.877 fm) — is resolved in Tetryonics by the non-spherical geometry of the proton. Three distinct geometric quantities characterize the proton:

- Z-axis height (gauge length): l_q = 1.000 fm
- Inscribed radius: (√3/2) × l_q = 0.866 fm
- Circumscribed side length: L = (2/√3) × l_q = 1.155 fm

Both experimental values fall within the geometric bounds defined by [0.866, 1.155] fm, with different experiments sampling different geometric projections.

### 7.2 Flatness of the Universe

The spatial flatness of the universe (Ω_k ≈ 0 per WMAP and Planck satellite observations) is derived geometrically in Tetryonics. The equilateral ZPF tessellation requires exactly six triangles meeting at each vertex (6 × 60° = 360°), producing zero angular defect and therefore zero Gaussian curvature. The cosmological parameter Ω_k = 0 is a geometric necessity, not a fine-tuning requirement.

### 7.3 Planck Constant Discrepancy

Abraham predicts that the SI value of Planck's constant as measured by the Kibble Balance is geometrically deficient because the Kibble Balance measurement principle omits the two-dimensional equilateral correction factor. The predicted geometric ratio is:

h_Tetryonic / h_SI = 1.0005075

This predicts a 0.05075% systematic error in all masses currently defined via SI Planck constant, including the kilogram.

### 7.4 Sub-Quark Structure

Abraham predicts that quarks are composed of three Tetryons, each carrying one-third the fractional charge of the parent quark. This implies sub-quark structure detectable at sufficient collision energy via three-lobed geometric cross-section distributions in deep inelastic scattering experiments.

### 7.5 Photon Composites

Abraham predicts the photon is a two-quantum composite, splitting into constituent half-photon bosons (single ZPF quanta) under sufficiently strong electromagnetic field geometries. Partial support for this prediction is claimed through orbital angular momentum (OAM) experiments demonstrating that photons carry orbital angular momentum in discrete integer multiples.

