## 9. Reception

Tetryonics has not been formally reviewed in mainstream peer-reviewed physics journals beyond the IJSRP publication. The framework sits outside the standard model of particle physics and general relativity as currently practised in academic institutions. Its claims of resolving open problems including the Yang-Mills mass gap, the proton radius puzzle, quantum gravity, and the measurement problem of the kilogram have not been independently verified by the broader physics community.

The works have attracted an independent research community, including collaborators who have produced formal mathematical proofs of specific components of the framework. James M. Moore formally proved the bijectivity of the Gnomon Coordinate Function and published a derivation of Planck's constant from CMB scattering plane geometry building on Abraham's corrected value. Richard A. Blankenship contributed the insertion of Tetryonic sequential labeling into the Pythagorean Gnomon grid and dimensional analysis of the matrix structure.

Abraham has described his work as a bottom-up geometric unification of physical science, in contrast to the top-down fragmented approach of modern theoretical physics. He has stated that the framework makes no free-parameter adjustments — all physical constants follow from the invariant 10⁻¹⁵ metre gauge and the equilateral geometric constraint.

